  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is that the `habeas-requirements` skill \"MUST direct agents not to commit requirements or write implementation or test code until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\" It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type or delegation. The obligation is about what the skill document says. The supplied root, `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, is that document, so it is the artifact the clause applies to.\n\nThe root does carry the directive. In `section[Habeas%20requirements]/section[Workflow]`, step 4 has a **Gate:** \"no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\" It adds that a general request to build the feature does not count as agreement. Step 5 (Commit) comes after that gate and commits only the agreed text, before any code. The wording matches the requirement's three outcomes (agreed, rejected, deferred) and both prohibitions (commit, and write implementation or test code). I found no structural gap and no missing root.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one observable obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents not to commit requirements or write implementation or test code until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_gates_commit_and_code_on_every_group_being_decided]` carries the `{test}` reference. It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`. That helper reads the bundled skill through `bundled_skill`, which joins `SKILL.md` and its reference files and collapses whitespace. The helper then asserts through `assert_text` that the text contains this exact gate sentence: \"**Gate:** no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\".\n\nThe sentence covers each element of the obligation:\n- It forbids implementation code and test code.\n- It forbids commits.\n- Its condition is that every group is agreed to, rejected, or deferred.\n\nThe assertion fails if the sentence is removed or weakened. A text-content check is the right kind of test for a requirement about what the skill directs. No other behavioral obligation needs test evidence, and there is no acknowledgment to consider.",
